+'\" t
+.\"     Title: login.conf
+.\"    Author: [see the "AUTHOR(S)" section]
+.\" Generator: Asciidoctor 2.0.23
+.\"      Date: 2026-10-16
+.\"    Manual: File formats
+.\"    Source: util-linux 2.42.4
+.\"  Language: English
+.\"
+.TH "LOGIN.CONF" "5" "2026-10-16" "util\-linux 2.42.4" "File formats"
+.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
+.el       .ds Aq '
+.ss \n[.ss] 0
+.nh
+.ad l
+.de URL
+\fI\\$2\fP <\\$1>\\$3
+..
+.als MTO URL
+.if \n[.g] \{\
+.  mso www.tmac
+.  am URL
+.    ad l
+.  .
+.  am MTO
+.    ad l
+.  .
+.  LINKSTYLE blue R < >
+.\}
+.SH "NAME"
+login.conf \- login configuration files
+.SH "SYNOPSIS"
+.sp
+\fI/etc/login.conf\fP
+\fI~/.login_conf\fP
+.SH "DESCRIPTION"
+.sp
+The \fI/etc/login.conf\fP and \fI~/.login_conf\fP files contain configuration instructions for \fBlogin\fP(1).
+The former applies system\-wide.
+The latter applies to the user(s) whose home directory it is in.
+.sp
+Per\-user capabilities settings override system\-wide settings.
+.sp
+Each file comprises comment lines starting with \*(Aq#\*(Aq, blank lines between records, and record lines.
+Records may optionally be continued on following lines by ending a record line with \*(Aq\(rs\*(Aq.
+.sp
+A record is a series of capabilities, as in a \fBtermcap\fP(5) file, separated by \*(Aq:\*(Aq.
+Leading whitespace before the name of the capability is ignored.
+.sp
+Capabilities take four forms:
+.sp
+.RS 4
+.ie n \{\
+\h'-04' 1.\h'+01'\c
+.\}
+.el \{\
+.  sp -1
+.  IP " 1." 4.2
+.\}
+A single identifier.  This denotes a boolean capability which is set to \fBtrue\fP.
+.RE
+.sp
+.RS 4
+.ie n \{\
+\h'-04' 2.\h'+01'\c
+.\}
+.el \{\
+.  sp -1
+.  IP " 2." 4.2
+.\}
+A single identifier followed by \*(Aq@\*(Aq.  This denotes a boolean capability which is set to \fBfalse\fP.
+.RE
+.sp
+.RS 4
+.ie n \{\
+\h'-04' 3.\h'+01'\c
+.\}
+.el \{\
+.  sp -1
+.  IP " 3." 4.2
+.\}
+A single identifier followed by \*(Aq=\*(Aq and a value.  This denotes a string capability.
+.RE
+.sp
+.RS 4
+.ie n \{\
+\h'-04' 4.\h'+01'\c
+.\}
+.el \{\
+.  sp -1
+.  IP " 4." 4.2
+.\}
+Whitespace.  Usually this is used to continue records over multiple lines without splitting the capability value.  These capabilities are ignored.
+.RE
+.SH "CAPABILITIES"
+.sp
+The very first capability in a record is special.
+It is a vertical bar (\*(Aq|\*(Aq) separated list of the login class names that the record applies to.
+All of the other capabilities are either string capabilities or boolean capabilities.
+.SS "BOOLEAN"
+.sp
+\fBhushlogin\fP
+.RS 4
+This is equivalent to having a \fI~/.hushlogin\fP file, and causes \fBlogin\fP(8) not to print various welcome messages upon login.
+.RE
+.SS "STRING"
+.sp
+String settings specify either filenames to use for messages or values to set environment variables to.
+.sp
+\fBcopyright\fP
+.RS 4
+The name of the file containing a copyright message to print on login.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBwelcome\fP
+.RS 4
+The name of the file containing a welcome ("message of the day") message to print on login.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBlogin_prompt\fP
+.RS 4
+The login prompt string to display
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBpasswd_prompt\fP
+.RS 4
+The login prompt string to display
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBeditor\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CREDITOR\fP environment variable to.
+This conventionally names a line\-mode terminal\-based editor.
+If this capability is not set, the default is \fBex\fP(8).
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBvisual\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRVISUAL\fP environment variable to.
+This conventionally names a full\-screen terminal\-based editor.
+If this capability is not set, the default is \fBvi\fP(8).
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBpager\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRPAGER\fP environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is either \fBpg\fP(8) from \fButil\-linux\fP or \fBmore\fP(1).
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBmanpager\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRMANPAGER\fP environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is either \fBpg\fP(8) from \fButil\-linux\fP or \fBmore\fP(1).
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBwww\-browser\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRWWWBROWSER\fP environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is either \fBwww\-browser\fP(1).
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBtimezone\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRTZ\fP environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is \f(CRUTC\fP.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBpath\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRPATH\fP environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBmanpath\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRMANPATH\fP environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBtermpath\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRTERMPATH\fP environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBterminfopath\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRTERMINFO_DIRS\fP environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBmanpath\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRMANPATH\fP environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBcharset\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRMM_CHARSET\fP environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is \f(CRUTF\-8\fP.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBlang\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRLANG\fP environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is \f(CRC.UTF\-8\fP.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBshell\fP
+.RS 4
+The value to set the \f(CRSHELL\fP environment variable to.
+.RE
+.sp
+\fBsetenv\fP
+.RS 4
+This specifies multiple environment variables to set not covered by other specialized settings.
+.RE
+.sp
+All string variables first have transformations performed on their values to allow various meta\-characters to be included in the value.
+This is a generic convention for capabilities files.
+The following escape sequences are recognized.
+.RS 3
+.ll -.6i
+.sp
+.if n .RS 4
+.nf
+.fam C
+\(rs0\&   NUL\&   null character
+\(rsb\&   BS\&    backspace
+\(rsr\&   CR\&    carriage return
+\(rsc\&   :\&     colon
+\(rse\&   ESC\&   escape
+\(rst\&   TAB\&   forward tab
+\(rsn\&   LF\&    line feed
+\(rsf\&   FF\&    form\-feed
+\(rs\(rs\&   \(rs\&     backslash
+^C\&         a control character in caret notation
+.fam
+.fi
+.if n .RE
+.br
+.RE
+.ll
+.sp
+String capabilities whose values are subject to path processing are then split into a whitespace\-separated list of path entries.
+A single run of whitespace is converted to a colon (\*(Aq:\*(Aq).
+A tilde (\*(Aq~\*(Aq) is converted into the value of the \f(CRHOME\fP environment variable.
+A dollar symbol (\*(Aq$\*(Aq) is converted into the value of the \f(CRUSER\fP environment variable.
+A backslash can be used to escape whitespace, tildes, and dollar symbols; and has to be used to escape backslashes.
+(In combination with escape processing, this means that backslashes have to be written twice to escape tildes and suchlike, and written four times to become a single backslash.)
+.sp
+The \fBsetenv\fP capability value is treated as a comma\-separated list of \fBname\fP=\fBvalue\fP pairs.
+Tilde (\*(Aq~\*(Aq) and dollar sign (\*(Aq$\*(Aq) characters are expanded as with path processing.
+A backslash can be used to escape whitespace, tildes, dollar symbols, and commas; and has to be used to escape backslashes.
+The environment variable \fBname\fP is to be set to the value \fBvalue\fP; unless \fBvalue\fP is an empty string, which unsets the environment variable \fBname\fP.
+.SH "EXAMPLES"
+.sp
+The following is a typical example of an \fB/etc/login.conf\fP file:
+.sp
+.if n .RS 4
+.nf
+.fam C
+default:\(rs
+        :copyright=/etc/COPYRIGHT:\(rs
+        :welcome=/etc/motd:\(rs
+        :visual=vim:\(rs
+        :editor=ex:\(rs
+        :login_prompt=Login account\(rsc:\(rs
+        :passwd_prompt=Account password\(rsc:\(rs
+        :timezone=Europe/London:\(rs
+        :setenv=MAIL=/var/mail/$,BLOCKSIZE=K,CLICOLOR=1,MAILPATH=~/Maildir/new,ENV=~/.config/shrc:\(rs
+        :path=~/bin /usr/local/bin /usr/pkg/bin /usr/bin:\(rs
+        :termpath=/usr/share/misc/termcap:\(rs
+        :terminfopath=~/.terminfo /usr/local/etc/terminfo /etc/terminfo /usr/pkg/etc/terminfo /usr/share/misc/terminfo:\(rs
+        :manpath=/usr/local/man /usr/local/share/man /usr/pkg/man /usr/share/man /usr/man:\(rs
+        :charset=UTF\-8:\(rs
+        :lang=en_GB.UTF\-8:
+
+root:\(rs
+        :copyright=/etc/COPYRIGHT:\(rs
+        :welcome=/etc/motd:\(rs
+        :visual=vi:\(rs
+        :editor=ex:\(rs
+        :timezone=Europe/London:\(rs
+        :setenv=MAIL=/var/mail/$,BLOCKSIZE=K,CLICOLOR=1,MAILPATH=~/Maildir/new,ENV=~/.config/shrc:\(rs
+        :path=~/bin /usr/local/sbin /usr/local/bin /usr/pkg/sbin /usr/pkg/bin /usr/sbin /usr/bin /sbin /bin:\(rs
+        :termpath=/usr/share/misc/termcap:\(rs
+        :terminfopath=~/.terminfo /usr/local/etc/terminfo /etc/terminfo /usr/pkg/etc/terminfo /usr/share/misc/terminfo:\(rs
+        :manpath=/usr/local/man /usr/local/share/man /usr/pkg/man /usr/share/man /usr/man:\(rs
+        :charset=UTF\-8:\(rs
+        :lang=en_US.UTF\-8:
+.fam
+.fi
+.if n .RE
+.sp
+The following is a typical example of a \fB~/.login_conf\fP file:
+.sp
+.if n .RS 4
+.nf
+.fam C
+me:\(rs
+        :setenv=CC=clang,CXX=clang++:\(rs
+        :visual=nvi:\(rs
+        :editor=nex:\(rs
+        :timezone=America/Los_Angeles:\(rs
+        :shell=/usr/pkg/bin/zsh:\(rs
+        :hushlogin:\(rs
+        :path=~/bin /usr/local/sbin /usr/local/bin /usr/pkg/sbin /usr/pkg/bin /usr/sbin /usr/bin /command:
+.fam
+.fi
+.if n .RE
+.SH "HISTORY"
+.sp
+\fBlogin.conf\fP files come from FreeBSD and NetBSD.
+In NetBSD, the general syntax of capabilities files, use for more than just \fBlogin.conf\fP, is given in \fBcapfile\fP(5).
+.sp
+\fButil\-linux\fP patches to process a subset of the FreeBSD format were written in 2026 by Jonathan de Boyne Pollard.
+.SH "BUGS"
+.sp
+The original FreeBSD file format is two Berkeley DB databases, \fI/etc/login.conf.db\fP and \fI~/.login_conf.db\fP, compiled from the text files for speed of lookup, with a \fBcap_mkdb\fP(1) tool.
+\fButil\-linux\fP does not yet have such tool; nor does it use GDBM files.
+The current \fBlogin\fP(8) program reads the source text files instead.
+.sp
+The BSD\(cqs \fItc\fP capability is not implemented in the \fButil\-linux\fP \fBlogin\fP(8) program.
+Every record has to be given in full.
+.sp
+Despite the examples, FreeBSD\(cqs \fIwelcome\fP, \fIcopyright\fP, \fIlogin_prompt\fP, and \fIpasswd_prompt\fP capabilities are not implemented in the \fButil\-linux\fP \fBlogin\fP(8) program.
+.sp
+The \fBpasswd\fP(5) file does not have a field for a login class.
+The only recognized login classes are \fBdefault\fP, \fBroot\fP, and \fBme\fP.
+.sp
+FreeBSD\(cqs and NetBSD\(cqs \fBlogin.conf\fP(5) manual pages define a lot more settings, which are not implemented here.
+Many of them are not implemented on FreeBSD and NetBSD either.
+.sp
+Numeric capabilities are not implemented.
