+:doctype: manpage
+:man manual: File formats
+:man source: util-linux {release-version}
+:page-layout: base
+:configfile: login.conf
+
+== NAME
+
+login.conf - login configuration files
+
+== SYNOPSIS
+
+_/etc/login.conf_
+_~/.login_conf_
+
+== DESCRIPTION
+
+The _/etc/login.conf_ and _~/.login_conf_ files contain configuration instructions for *login*(1).
+The former applies system-wide.
+The latter applies to the user(s) whose home directory it is in.
+
+Per-user capabilities settings override system-wide settings.
+
+Each file comprises comment lines starting with '#', blank lines between records, and record lines.
+Records may optionally be continued on following lines by ending a record line with '\'.
+
+A record is a series of capabilities, as in a *termcap*(5) file, separated by ':'.
+Leading whitespace before the name of the capability is ignored.
+
+Capabilities take four forms:
+
+1. A single identifier.  This denotes a boolean capability which is set to *true*.
+2. A single identifier followed by '@'.  This denotes a boolean capability which is set to *false*.
+3. A single identifier followed by '=' and a value.  This denotes a string capability.
+4. Whitespace.  Usually this is used to continue records over multiple lines without splitting the capability value.  These capabilities are ignored.
+
+== CAPABILITIES
+
+The very first capability in a record is special.
+It is a vertical bar ('|') separated list of the login class names that the record applies to.
+All of the other capabilities are either string capabilities or boolean capabilities.
+
+=== BOOLEAN
+
+*hushlogin*::
+This is equivalent to having a _~/.hushlogin_ file, and causes *login*(8) not to print various welcome messages upon login.
+
+=== STRING
+
+String settings specify either filenames to use for messages or values to set environment variables to.
+
+*copyright*::
+The name of the file containing a copyright message to print on login.
+
+*welcome*::
+The name of the file containing a welcome ("message of the day") message to print on login.
+
+*login_prompt*::
+The login prompt string to display
+
+*passwd_prompt*::
+The login prompt string to display
+
+*editor*::
+The value to set the `EDITOR` environment variable to.
+This conventionally names a line-mode terminal-based editor.
+If this capability is not set, the default is *ex*(8).
+
+*visual*::
+The value to set the `VISUAL` environment variable to.
+This conventionally names a full-screen terminal-based editor.
+If this capability is not set, the default is *vi*(8).
+
+*pager*::
+The value to set the `PAGER` environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is either *pg*(8) from *util-linux* or *more*(1).
+
+*manpager*::
+The value to set the `MANPAGER` environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is either *pg*(8) from *util-linux* or *more*(1).
+
+*www-browser*::
+The value to set the `WWWBROWSER` environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is either *www-browser*(1).
+
+*timezone*::
+The value to set the `TZ` environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is `UTC`.
+
+*path*::
+The value to set the `PATH` environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+
+*manpath*::
+The value to set the `MANPATH` environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+
+*termpath*::
+The value to set the `TERMPATH` environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+
+*terminfopath*::
+The value to set the `TERMINFO_DIRS` environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+
+*manpath*::
+The value to set the `MANPATH` environment variable to.
+This is subject to path processing after escape sequence processing.
+
+*charset*::
+The value to set the `MM_CHARSET` environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is `UTF-8`.
+
+*lang*::
+The value to set the `LANG` environment variable to.
+If this capability is not set, the default is `C.UTF-8`.
+
+*shell*::
+The value to set the `SHELL` environment variable to.
+
+*setenv*::
+This specifies multiple environment variables to set not covered by other specialized settings.
+
+All string variables first have transformations performed on their values to allow various meta-characters to be included in the value.
+This is a generic convention for capabilities files.
+The following escape sequences are recognized.
+____
+ \0   NUL   null character
+ \b   BS    backspace
+ \r   CR    carriage return
+ \c   :     colon
+ \e   ESC   escape
+ \t   TAB   forward tab
+ \n   LF    line feed
+ \f   FF    form-feed
+ \\   \     backslash
+ ^C         a control character in caret notation
+____
+
+String capabilities whose values are subject to path processing are then split into a whitespace-separated list of path entries.
+A single run of whitespace is converted to a colon (':').
+A tilde ('~') is converted into the value of the `HOME` environment variable.
+A dollar symbol ('$') is converted into the value of the `USER` environment variable.
+A backslash can be used to escape whitespace, tildes, and dollar symbols; and has to be used to escape backslashes.
+(In combination with escape processing, this means that backslashes have to be written twice to escape tildes and suchlike, and written four times to become a single backslash.)
+
+The *setenv* capability value is treated as a comma-separated list of *name*=*value* pairs.
+Tilde ('~') and dollar sign ('$') characters are expanded as with path processing.
+A backslash can be used to escape whitespace, tildes, dollar symbols, and commas; and has to be used to escape backslashes.
+The environment variable *name* is to be set to the value *value*; unless *value* is an empty string, which unsets the environment variable *name*.
+
+== EXAMPLES
+
+The following is a typical example of an */etc/login.conf* file:
+
+....
+default:\
+        :copyright=/etc/COPYRIGHT:\
+        :welcome=/etc/motd:\
+        :visual=vim:\
+        :editor=ex:\
+        :login_prompt=Login account\c:\
+        :passwd_prompt=Account password\c:\
+        :timezone=Europe/London:\
+        :setenv=MAIL=/var/mail/$,BLOCKSIZE=K,CLICOLOR=1,MAILPATH=~/Maildir/new,ENV=~/.config/shrc:\
+        :path=~/bin /usr/local/bin /usr/pkg/bin /usr/bin:\
+        :termpath=/usr/share/misc/termcap:\
+	:terminfopath=~/.terminfo /usr/local/etc/terminfo /etc/terminfo /usr/pkg/etc/terminfo /usr/share/misc/terminfo:\
+        :manpath=/usr/local/man /usr/local/share/man /usr/pkg/man /usr/share/man /usr/man:\
+        :charset=UTF-8:\
+        :lang=en_GB.UTF-8:
+
+root:\
+        :copyright=/etc/COPYRIGHT:\
+        :welcome=/etc/motd:\
+        :visual=vi:\
+        :editor=ex:\
+        :timezone=Europe/London:\
+        :setenv=MAIL=/var/mail/$,BLOCKSIZE=K,CLICOLOR=1,MAILPATH=~/Maildir/new,ENV=~/.config/shrc:\
+        :path=~/bin /usr/local/sbin /usr/local/bin /usr/pkg/sbin /usr/pkg/bin /usr/sbin /usr/bin /sbin /bin:\
+        :termpath=/usr/share/misc/termcap:\
+	:terminfopath=~/.terminfo /usr/local/etc/terminfo /etc/terminfo /usr/pkg/etc/terminfo /usr/share/misc/terminfo:\
+        :manpath=/usr/local/man /usr/local/share/man /usr/pkg/man /usr/share/man /usr/man:\
+        :charset=UTF-8:\
+        :lang=en_US.UTF-8:
+
+....
+
+The following is a typical example of a *~/.login_conf* file:
+
+....
+me:\
+        :setenv=CC=clang,CXX=clang++:\
+        :visual=nvi:\
+        :editor=nex:\
+        :timezone=America/Los_Angeles:\
+        :shell=/usr/pkg/bin/zsh:\
+        :hushlogin:\
+        :path=~/bin /usr/local/sbin /usr/local/bin /usr/pkg/sbin /usr/pkg/bin /usr/sbin /usr/bin /command:
+
+....
+
+== HISTORY
+
+*login.conf* files come from FreeBSD and NetBSD.
+In NetBSD, the general syntax of capabilities files, use for more than just *login.conf*, is given in *capfile*(5).
+
+*util-linux* patches to process a subset of the FreeBSD format were written in 2026 by Jonathan de Boyne Pollard.
+
+== BUGS
+
+The original FreeBSD file format is two Berkeley DB databases, _/etc/login.conf.db_ and _~/.login_conf.db_, compiled from the text files for speed of lookup, with a *cap_mkdb*(1) tool.
+*util-linux* does not yet have such tool; nor does it use GDBM files.
+The current *login*(8) program reads the source text files instead.
+
+The BSD's _tc_ capability is not implemented in the *util-linux* *login*(8) program.
+Every record has to be given in full.
+
+Despite the examples, FreeBSD's _welcome_, _copyright_, _login_prompt_, and _passwd_prompt_ capabilities are not implemented in the *util-linux* *login*(8) program.
+
+The *passwd*(5) file does not have a field for a login class.
+The only recognized login classes are *default*, *root*, and *me*.
+
+FreeBSD's and NetBSD's *login.conf*(5) manual pages define a lot more settings, which are not implemented here.
+Many of them are not implemented on FreeBSD and NetBSD either.
+
+Numeric capabilities are not implemented.
+Setting resource limits is not yet supported.
+
